WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:11.960
Brazil is the country of the future… and
it seems to keep being so forever.

00:00:11.960 --> 00:00:15.961
Lately we’ve heard a lot about the country’s
corruption scandals, the economic crisis it

00:00:15.961 --> 00:00:18.900
is going through or the street violence in
cities like Rio de Janeiro.

00:00:18.900 --> 00:00:22.750
But there is something even more important
about this country! Something that doesn’t

00:00:22.750 --> 00:00:27.180
appear very much in the media.

00:00:27.180 --> 00:00:33.250
Brazil is the MOST expensive country in all
of South America. Yep. You heard that right.

00:00:33.250 --> 00:00:37.210
If you go Shopping in a city like Sao Paolo
or Rio de Janeiro, you’d better be ready

00:00:37.210 --> 00:00:42.480
to clean your wallet because it will be as
expensive as New York or London.

00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:46.570
The Brazilians even have a name for this.
They call it ‘CUSTO BRAZIL’. This is the

00:00:46.570 --> 00:00:49.360
especial overcost you have to pay for living
in the country of samba and carnaval.

00:00:49.360 --> 00:00:54.460
Here is an example. The Big Mac Burger you
get from McDonalds. If you’ve been subscribed

00:00:54.460 --> 00:00:58.710
to VisualPolitik for a long time, you already
know that the economists use the so called

00:00:58.710 --> 00:01:03.010
BIG MAC INDEX to measure the cost of living
in a country.

00:01:03.010 --> 00:01:09.790
For example, in SWITZERLAND, one of the wealthiest
countries in the World, a Big Mac costs the

00:01:09.790 --> 00:01:11.790
equivalent of 6.75 bucks.

00:01:11.790 --> 00:01:17.200
Nonetheless, in PERU, a poorer country, the
burger costs only 3.23 dollars. Fair enough,

00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:23.420
right? Well, in Brazil, the Big Mac costs
5.10 USD. It’s more expensive than in Denmark,

00:01:23.420 --> 00:01:25.250
UK or Canada.

00:01:25.250 --> 00:01:29.820
According to EXPATISTAN.COM, a website, Sao
Paolo is the most expensive city in South

00:01:29.820 --> 00:01:38.210
America, with prices as high as those of Houston,
Texas, or Berlin, Germany.

00:01:38.210 --> 00:01:43.689
And now you might wonder… Given the high
cost of living, how high are Brazilian salaries?

00:01:43.689 --> 00:01:47.130
Are they wealthier than, say, Colombians or
Chileans?

00:01:47.130 --> 00:01:48.240
Absolutely not.

00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:53.649
The average wage in Brazil is less than 600
USD a month. Argentinians and Chileans are

00:01:53.649 --> 00:01:55.079
better off in this regard.

00:01:55.079 --> 00:01:59.939
So the real question here is Why is Brazil
so expensive? What are the reasons why a middle

00:01:59.939 --> 00:02:03.220
income country has the prices of a wealthy
one?

00:02:03.220 --> 00:02:07.729
Today we are going to answer this question.
And believe me, this explains, partly, the

00:02:07.729 --> 00:02:11.750
Brazilean crisis too. Let’s take a look
at this.

00:02:11.750 --> 00:02:13.360
AN ISOLATED ECONOMY

00:02:13.360 --> 00:02:17.500
Many of you might think Brazil is a country
open to foreign trade, right? At the end of

00:02:17.500 --> 00:02:22.590
the day, many foreign companies like MasterCard
or Barclays have a strong presence on this

00:02:22.590 --> 00:02:26.660
country.

00:02:26.660 --> 00:02:33.550
Nonetheless, Brazil has the highest tariffs
in South America.

00:02:33.550 --> 00:02:35.150
And what does this mean?

00:02:35.150 --> 00:02:40.261
Well… imagine we have a cell phone manufacturer
in America and we want to sell in the Brazilian

00:02:40.261 --> 00:02:45.250
market. Since our product is made abroad,
we must sell it with a special tax that can

00:02:45.250 --> 00:02:48.060
reach up to 35% of its market price.

00:02:48.060 --> 00:02:55.330
As you can imagine, this kind of policy has
a big impact on everyday life…

00:02:55.330 --> 00:02:58.310
Nintendo exits Brazilian market due to high&nbsp;tariffs

00:02:58.310 --> 00:03:03.300
Yep. You heard that right. During those years,
Brazilians who wanted to buy a WII or a Nintendo

00:03:03.300 --> 00:03:06.900
DS had to travel to Uruguay in order to find
one.

00:03:06.900 --> 00:03:10.830
Interestingly, this was a great advantage
for Nintendo’s biggest rivals, especially

00:03:10.830 --> 00:03:16.630
Microsoft and the XBOX. Do you know why? Microsoft
has a factory in Brazil where the XBOX ONE

00:03:16.630 --> 00:03:20.890
is made. This means, they don’t have to
import it and, therefore, it don’t have

00:03:20.890 --> 00:03:26.680
those high tariffs.

00:03:26.680 --> 00:03:30.560
And yes, I know what you are thinking! High
tariffs are cool because they force companies

00:03:30.560 --> 00:03:34.470
to build factories in your country and employ
your citizens, right?

00:03:34.470 --> 00:03:39.680
Well… think twice! When Microsoft opens
that Brazilian factory, they still need to

00:03:39.680 --> 00:03:44.970
import all the machines and materials needed
to manufacture that XBOX ONE.

00:03:44.970 --> 00:03:51.040
Plastics, semiconductors, robots… many of
which are made somewhere else.

00:03:51.040 --> 00:03:56.430
And, if they are made somewhere else, they
have those special tariffs.

00:03:56.430 --> 00:04:00.820
And guess who pays for this overcost? The
final consumer!

00:04:00.820 --> 00:04:04.849
This explains why so many products manufactured
In Brazil are so expensive.

00:04:04.849 --> 00:04:07.209
Want an example? Take the VOLKSWAGEN GOL.

00:04:07.209 --> 00:04:16.501
In Mexico City, this car costs around 15,000
USD. In Sao Paolo, it costs 25,000 USD. All

00:04:16.501 --> 00:04:21.440
of this, despite the fact that this specific
model of automobile is manufactured in Sao

00:04:21.440 --> 00:04:22.440
Paulo.

00:04:22.440 --> 00:04:31.360
But wait a minute because this is not the
only reason to explain this CUSTO BRAZIL!

00:04:31.360 --> 00:04:34.610
LOGYSTIC “CUSTO”

00:04:34.610 --> 00:04:38.490
Imagine a SOY agriculture in the state of
PARA, in the North of the country. If he wants

00:04:38.490 --> 00:04:43.811
to send his SOY to Sao Paolo, he must carry
it on trucks through the highway BR-163. This

00:04:43.811 --> 00:04:49.580
highway has over 1000 kilometers… and 100
are not even paved. A simple rainy day is

00:04:49.580 --> 00:04:51.930
enough to keep those trucks stuck in the mud.

00:04:51.930 --> 00:04:59.020
And this is just one example! In fact, Brazil
has some of the worst infrastructure on the

00:04:59.020 --> 00:05:00.490
planet.

00:05:00.490 --> 00:05:04.979
Even INDIA has better roads. So, to make a
comparison, anytime an American farmer sells

00:05:04.979 --> 00:05:09.100
100 USD in Soy, he has to spend 9 USD in transport.

00:05:09.100 --> 00:05:12.220
The same farmer in Brazil would have to spend
25 USD.

00:05:12.220 --> 00:05:16.360
Since they have no good roads, Brazilians
have to invest in expensive trucks that break

00:05:16.360 --> 00:05:19.460
down quickly and make the cost even more expensive.

00:05:19.460 --> 00:05:25.210
And, of course, all of this means higher and
higher prices for their Brazilian customers…

00:05:25.210 --> 00:05:29.800
But now you might wonder… Why is the Brazilian
government so reluctant to pay for infrastructure?

00:05:29.800 --> 00:05:34.570
At the end of the day, Brazil has state of
the art engineering companies. Companies like…

00:05:34.570 --> 00:05:36.600
ODEBRECHT group.

00:05:36.600 --> 00:05:44.949
If you see this video from a South American
country, I’m sure this name will ring a

00:05:44.949 --> 00:05:45.949
bell.

00:05:45.949 --> 00:05:49.300
Odebrecht was the biggest engineering and
construction company in the region.

00:05:49.300 --> 00:05:54.259
And I say ‘WAS’ because they had the biggest
corruption scandal in the history of the continent.

00:05:54.259 --> 00:05:59.150
So now the future of this firm is pretty dim.

00:05:59.150 --> 00:06:03.300
But this story explains pretty well why Brazil
has such poor infrastructure.

00:06:03.300 --> 00:06:07.660
At the end of the day, part of this money
goes toward bribes and corruption.

00:06:07.660 --> 00:06:12.520
But not only that! When the Brazilian government
wanted to modernize their country… what

00:06:12.520 --> 00:06:20.160
did they do? Exactly! The Olympic games and
FIFA world cup!

00:06:20.160 --> 00:06:24.480
So… today, Brazilian cities can congratulate
themselves for having the most beautiful sport

00:06:24.480 --> 00:06:28.280
arenas, with high end technology and luxurious
decoration.

00:06:28.280 --> 00:06:32.110
Taking a walk in the center of Sao Paolo feels
like being in a sci fy movie.

00:06:32.110 --> 00:06:37.100
But outside of this metropolis, the railroads
are abandoned, the highways are unpaved and

00:06:37.100 --> 00:06:41.520
several neighbourhoods don’t have a single
bus line that connects them with the city

00:06:41.520 --> 00:06:43.310
center.

00:06:43.310 --> 00:06:49.419
Brazil’s woeful infrastructure costs the
economy as much as 48 &nbsp;billion USD &nbsp;per

00:06:49.419 --> 00:06:50.419
year

00:06:50.419 --> 00:06:55.600
Of course, this affects the prices for Brazilian
consumers but also for Worldwide consumers

00:06:55.600 --> 00:06:57.490
who want to buy Brazilian goods.

00:06:57.490 --> 00:07:00.590
But we will talk about this in another video.

00:07:00.590 --> 00:07:05.020
Because now the question is… How can someone
who makes 600 bucks a month spend 25 bucks

00:07:05.020 --> 00:07:08.210
on a pizza at an average restaurant in Sao
Paulo?

00:07:08.210 --> 00:07:14.880
Well… I’ll tell you how…

00:07:14.880 --> 00:07:15.880
CREDIT-A-HOLICS

00:07:15.880 --> 00:07:19.569
For decades, Brazil had some of the highest
inflation in the World.

00:07:19.569 --> 00:07:25.330
During the 80s and the 90s, they had more
than a &nbsp;6000% inflation rate. This means

00:07:25.330 --> 00:07:30.729
if you had a beer at a Rio de Janeiro bar,
in 1980, you would pay 100 CRUCEROS. The CRUCERO

00:07:30.729 --> 00:07:35.570
was the Brazilian currency back then. One
year later, that same beer would cost 600,000

00:07:35.570 --> 00:07:45.540
CRUCEROS. As you can imagine, that hyperinflation
created unique consumer behavior. Something

00:07:45.540 --> 00:07:47.169
different from the rest of the world.

00:07:47.169 --> 00:07:51.879
In order to survive, Brazilian shops got used
to selling with installments. And this is

00:07:51.879 --> 00:07:53.169
still a thing today.

00:07:53.169 --> 00:07:56.810
If you go to any Brazilian shop, you can see
that, next to the price, it says something

00:07:56.810 --> 00:08:03.110
like 10x, 12x or 20x. This number and this
x means you can pay that product on 10, 12

00:08:03.110 --> 00:08:05.830
or 20 installments.

00:08:05.830 --> 00:08:13.110
In other words… the question many Brazilians
ask themselves is not whether this TV or this

00:08:13.110 --> 00:08:17.680
suit is more or less expensive, but if they
can pay for it during the next 2 years.

00:08:17.680 --> 00:08:24.889
And, as you can imagine, when you buy something
in instalments, it is more expensive. For

00:08:24.889 --> 00:08:30.449
example, on the screen we are showing you
a smartphone on a Brazilian ecommerce website.

00:08:30.449 --> 00:08:35.690
If you buy it at once, it costs 632 RAIS,
which equals to 202 USD at the moment we made

00:08:35.690 --> 00:08:36.690
this video.

00:08:36.690 --> 00:08:40.909
Nonetheless, if you buy it with instalments,
it costs 217 USD. That’s 7% extra that we

00:08:40.909 --> 00:08:43.039
have to add to the rest of the costs we already
mentioned.

00:08:43.039 --> 00:08:49.100
So as you can imagine, your average Brazilian
is in debt all the time. And this has a consequence.

00:08:49.100 --> 00:08:54.120
&nbsp;
Nearly 60% of Brazilians in Installment Debt

00:08:54.120 --> 00:08:57.691
But wait a minute because this is not the
worst of it! In the latest year, something

00:08:57.691 --> 00:09:02.980
has happened in Brazil that has turned CUSTO
BRAZIL into a real nightmare.

00:09:02.980 --> 00:09:05.640
CREDIT CARD HELL

00:09:05.640 --> 00:09:10.111
At the beginning of the 2000s, Brazil was
the perfect market for foreign banks. Big

00:09:10.111 --> 00:09:15.590
financial firms like SANTANDER or CREDIT SUISSE
started investing in the country.

00:09:15.590 --> 00:09:20.810
Don’t forget we are talking about a 200
million person country, that was on the rise,

00:09:20.810 --> 00:09:23.391
where prices were high and people were used
to paying for things on instalments.

00:09:23.391 --> 00:09:28.540
In other words, Brazil was a paradise for
selling credit cards.

00:09:28.540 --> 00:09:38.769
“Since 2005, the brazilian credit card market
has grown more than 20% year after year”

00:09:38.769 --> 00:09:44.081
By the year 2012, Brazilians had an average
of 2 credit cards per person. And oh boy!

00:09:44.081 --> 00:09:46.290
They knew how to use them!

00:09:46.290 --> 00:09:50.340
Brazil was the country with the most credit
payments all across South America. Nonetheless…

00:09:50.340 --> 00:09:53.940
the happiness didn’t last long.
&nbsp;

00:09:53.940 --> 00:09:58.940
Brazilian consumer default on credit cards
has reached 27% in April 2012

00:09:58.940 --> 00:10:04.800
In that very same year, 2012, the alarms started
to sound in all those Brazilian banks.

00:10:04.800 --> 00:10:08.839
The country was in the middle of a crisis,
with rising unemployment and declining salaries.

00:10:08.839 --> 00:10:12.170
And that made it hard for Brazilians to pay
back their debts.

00:10:12.170 --> 00:10:16.410
So… what happens when a bank is afraid of
getting defaulters? Exactly! They raise the

00:10:16.410 --> 00:10:17.550
interest rates.

00:10:17.550 --> 00:10:24.932
This is why, since september 2014, the interest
of those credit cards has reached figures

00:10:24.932 --> 00:10:25.932
around 400%.

00:10:25.932 --> 00:10:29.120
So imagine you use your credit card to buy
a TV screen that costs 1000 USD. You pay it

00:10:29.120 --> 00:10:30.120
in 12 instalments.

00:10:30.120 --> 00:10:34.440
Well, at the end of the day, counting the
interest rate of the credit card, the TV costs

00:10:34.440 --> 00:10:37.560
around 5000 USD. Impressive, isn’t it?

00:10:37.560 --> 00:10:42.251
And, don’t forget that the average wage
in Brazil is less than 600 USD a month. In

00:10:42.251 --> 00:10:46.721
other words… despite those anti poverty
measures made by the government… most of

00:10:46.721 --> 00:10:50.370
Brazilians are struggling to survive. And
worst of all, since they can’t save money,

00:10:50.370 --> 00:10:54.350
losing a job can be a real tragedy for many.

00:10:54.350 --> 00:11:03.480
Homeless population in Rio de Janeiro increases
150% in three years.

00:11:03.480 --> 00:11:08.200
So here is the question now… Can Brazil
get over this situation? Who could possibly

00:11:08.200 --> 00:11:13.399
reform this country? Please, leave your answer
on the comment section below.

